\title{CPU fallbacks and plan breaks: what cuplyr tells you, and how to hear it}
\description{
cuplyr runs a dplyr verb on the GPU whenever it can. Two things can happen
that you may want to know about. Both are silent by default, and both are
always \emph{signalled} as conditions, so a calling handler sees them whatever the
options say.
}
\section{CPU fallbacks}{

A verb (or part of one) that has no GPU implementation runs in dplyr on the
host: cuplyr collects the table, runs the real dplyr verb and uploads the
result. This is correct but slow for a big table. The condition has class
\code{cuplyr_cpu_fallback} and the fields
\itemize{
\item \code{verb}: the verb, as you wrote it (\code{"mutate"});
\item \code{expr}: the expression that forced the fallback (\code{"qnorm(x / 10)"}), or
\code{NA} when the call as a whole goes;
\item \code{reason}: why (\code{"`qnorm()` has no GPU implementation."}, a rowwise
table, a function of yours that shadows a native one, an unsupported
type, ...);
\item \code{rows}, \code{cols}, \code{bytes}: what is collected to the host (the table's
dimensions and an estimate of its size; a two-table verb adds both
tables).
}

\code{options(cuplyr.fallback = )} decides what happens next: \code{"silent"} (the
default), \code{"warn"} (a warning of class \code{cuplyr_warning_cpu_fallback}) or
\code{"error"} (an error of class \code{cuplyr_error_fallback}, for a script that must
stay on the GPU). \code{"warning"} is accepted as an alias of \code{"warn"}. The
message reads

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{cuplyr: mutate() fell back to CPU evaluation for: qnorm(x / 10)
i Reason: `qnorm()` has no GPU implementation.
i Collected 1,000 rows x 3 cols (about 24.4 kB) to run on the CPU.
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Plan breaks}{

A lazy table (\code{tbl_gpu(df, lazy = TRUE)}) collects its verbs into a plan and
runs it when you \code{compute()} or \code{collect()}. A few steps need the result of
the pending plan before it is asked for (a \code{summarise()} of an expression
such as \code{mean(x * y)}, an integer \code{max()}, a verb that leaves no column).
cuplyr then computes the plan early. This stays on the GPU and nothing is
collected, but the plan is not fused across that step. The condition has
class \code{cuplyr_plan_break} and the fields \code{verb}, \code{expr}, \code{reason} and
\code{steps} (the number of pending steps that ran early).

\code{options(cuplyr.plan_break = )} is \code{"silent"} (the default) or \code{"warn"} (a
warning of class \code{cuplyr_warning_plan_break}); \code{"warning"} is an alias of
\code{"warn"}. A plan break is never an error: nothing left the GPU.
}

\section{Invalid option values}{

Any other value of either option (including \code{"error"} for
\code{cuplyr.plan_break}) warns once per session and value, with class
\code{cuplyr_warning_invalid_option} (fields \code{option} and \code{value}), and the
default (\code{"silent"}) is used.
}

